## Wiring parameter contracts @#wiring-reference

A function parameter can accept an ordinary value, preserve the identity of a
named binding or describe a choice constructor. The parameter that directly
receives the argument determines which rule applies.

[table:
    [th: [: Parameter] [: What the caller supplies] [: What the function receives]]
    [td: [: `value T`] [: An ordinary argument of the expected type] [: The value under the ordinary access rules]]
    [td: [: `value Wire of T`] [: A named local or matching Wire parameter] [: Read access to the current value and the ability to forward the binding]]
    [td: [: `event Route of Message`] [: A complete constructor or matching Route parameter] [: The constructor and complete payload with no future input]]
    [td: [: `event Route of Input -> Message`] [: A constructor with one field left or a matching Route parameter] [: The constructor and the leading payload values with the final field reserved for `Input`]]
]

A message has an ordinary named choice type. A Route specifies how to build
one. Delivery needs a consumer and transport between tasks needs a channel.
A Route names neither a destination nor an event handler. These parameter contracts
introduce no general closures, source-level reference types or rendering policy.

### Reading `of`

The name before `of` determines what follows. A generic type such as
`Box of String` takes ordinary type arguments. `Wire` takes one value type.
`Route` takes a choice output type, optionally preceded by an input type
and `->`. The arrow belongs to the parameter. A function's return arrow
comes after its closing `|`. Moth defines no multi-input Route.

The compiler reserves the names `Wire` and `Route`. User declarations can't
override them or register another meaning for `of`. Ordinary generic arity,
inference and alias rules apply. Use an ordinary concrete type alias when the
underlying value needs a nested specialisation that the generic grammar
doesn't allow. `Box of Wire of T` can't create a container of Wires. Wiring
introduces no type values, higher-kinded types, explicit generic call
arguments or general inline nesting.

### Wire arguments @#wire-arguments

Pass a whole named mutable or immutable local, or forward an existing Wire
parameter unchanged. The receiver requests the binding's identity, so the
caller needs no marker and no `Wire()` constructor. In particular, `wire(...)`,
`<value>`, `<>value` and a `$` prefix don't form Wires.

A field, indexed element, literal, constructor call, function result,
`copy` or other computed expression can't supply an existing binding's
identity. A `#=` constant belongs to compile time. An ordinary value
parameter receives the value and can't recover the caller's binding. A new
local can supply its own identity, including when its initial value came
from another binding.

The underlying types must match exactly. An ordinary alias names the same
type. Wrapping a value in an optional or converting its numeric type would
change what the receiver reads, so neither conversion applies to a Wire.

Reassigning a mutable local keeps that binding's identity. Two locals can
hold equal values or share storage and still have different identities.
A fresh instance of a local in a later function call or loop iteration has
its own identity, even though it comes from the same line of source.

Reading a Wire into an ordinary local, passing it to a value parameter or
returning its underlying value performs an ordinary read. None of these
operations forwards identity or makes a deep copy. Aggregate reads can
create shared aliases. Where the ordinary copy rules permit it, `copy`
creates an independent value. See @../bindings/#explicit-copies (Explicit copies).

### Constructor arguments @#route-arguments

A Route parameter accepts a direct choice variant or an unchanged compatible
Route parameter. A previously built choice value, a function name, a closure
or an arbitrary expression can't supply the constructor. An expression can
supply a *field* of the constructor, including a function call that may fail.

[table:
    [th: [: Receiver] [: Supplied fields] [: Omitted fields]]
    [td: [: Ordinary choice value] [: Positional arguments followed by any named arguments] [: Fill from declared defaults. Require every field without a default.]]
    [td: [: Zero-input Route] [: The same positional and named constructor arguments] [: Fill from declared defaults to capture a complete payload.]]
    [td: [: One-input Route] [: Supply every field before the last one explicitly, by position and in declaration order, including fields with defaults.] [: Reserve exactly the final field for the future input, even when it has a default.]]
]

For a one-field payload, a one-input Route can use the bare variant path,
such as `FormMessage::NameChanged`. The future input supplies the whole
payload field. A unit variant such as `FormMessage::Submit` can supply a
zero-input Route. A unit variant takes no parentheses.

The compiler checks the immediate receiving parameter before it decides
whether a constructor may omit a required field. Assigning an incomplete
constructor to an ordinary local fails at the assignment. Passing that local
to a Route parameter later can't make the assignment valid.

Capture expressions run once, in written order, as part of evaluating the
call. Normal evaluation, failure and ownership rules apply. The Route keeps
the resulting values. A lookup in a capture therefore runs when the caller
forms the Route, even if no consumer ever handles an event. A runtime
capture remains runtime data even when the compiler knows the variant.

### Type identity and forwarding @#route-forwarding

Both Route types must match exactly to forward a parameter. This includes
the presence or absence of an input, its type when present and the output
choice. Two choices with identical variants still have distinct types.
Moth requires no common Message base type or trait and performs no variance
or callable-trait search.

A constructor used at a Route parameter doesn't become a first-class function.
A constructor can supply a generic helper's output choice through ordinary
immediate inference. Existing argument or result evidence can also establish
concrete types where the generic rules allow it. The compiler doesn't inspect
a distant function body to recover missing evidence. See
@../generics/#generic-inference (Generic inference).

An input can itself be a choice. Given the parent variant
`Editor | editor_id I32, message EditorMessage |`, the argument
`AppMessage::Editor(7)` satisfies `Route of EditorMessage -> AppMessage`.
It captures the editor ID and reserves the child message. A matching parameter
may forward this same Route. Connecting it to a second Route would require a
composition operation, which Wiring doesn't provide.

### Storage, access and lifetimes @#wiring-lifetimes

Wire and Route annotate function parameters only. Ordinary locals, struct
fields, choice payloads, collections, maps, aliases, defaults and return slots
can't hold these capabilities. A function can return an ordinary value read
from a Wire. Pipes around a struct's fields or a choice variant's payload
declare named storage on the struct and immutable data on the variant.
Neither position accepts a Wire or a Route.

Imports, exports and generic specialisation preserve all three parameter
contracts. Sharing an underlying type doesn't turn a Wire parameter into an
ordinary parameter or a Route into a choice value across a module boundary.

Wire identity grants neither exclusive access nor ownership of storage. An
immutable binding stays immutable. A mutation still requires ordinary
exclusive authority. Passing identity alone doesn't keep a value borrowed.
Actual reads and retained captures follow normal alias, copy, access and
lifetime rules. Retaining a reference requires proof that its source
outlives its consumer, even on a target with garbage collection.

A program can't store, return, compare, serialise or send either capability. Moth
provides no general operation to call or compose Routes. Forming one
neither registers an event nor sends a message.

### Consumers, builders and templates @#wiring-consumers

JavaScript/HTML supports synchronous Wire reads, Wire and Route forwarding
and constructor capture. HTML-Wasm supports Wires. If reachable code needs a
runtime capability the target doesn't support, the compiler reports a target
diagnostic before lowering it. This includes reachable Route formation on
HTML-Wasm.

String templates produce ordinary Strings. Composition, control flow, slots,
wrappers, formatting, folding and fragment insertion attach no Wire, Route
or rerender work. Resource and site-root pieces retain their usual meaning
until output placement resolves their text.

A future observer or event consumer would need its own retention and lifecycle
contract. Automatic observation, derived-state graphs, change handlers, two-way
binding, live templates, route invocation, event queues, scheduling, mount
lifecycles, rendering policy and channel bridges all require separate designs.
See @../progress (implementation status).

### Channel construction @#wiring-channels

Moth's accepted channel design uses another head before `of`. Channels still
await compiler and runtime support.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    reader, writer Channel of AppMessage = Channel()
]

The immediate annotation supplies the carried type to `Channel()`. It creates
one channel and returns a reader followed by a writer. Prefix `>>` dispatches
a task, infix `>>` sends a value and prefix `<<` receives an optional result.
An explicit send transfers an independent value graph under the async memory
contract. Wire identity and a Route can't travel as channel payloads. Endpoint
lifetimes, buffering and readiness selection belong to @../async (Channels and readiness).
